     The .orig files should contain what you had before running
patch.  The .rej files contain context diffs showing what parts
of the patch were rejected, and should resemble sections of the
patch.  In theory you can determine why a patch failed to apply
by examining those and comparing with the original contents; in
practice it's not always that simple.

     I suspect that you're actually just running into whitespace
problems (spaces where there should be tabs or vice versa), and
might be able to solve them by using ``patch -l'' (that's
lowercase -L, and I think it stands for "loose"; you can ask for
the same thing more verbosely via ``patch --ignore-whitespace'').

     The fact that the new files are larger probably means that
the originals used Unix-style newline (single line feed character)
for end of line, and the ones written by patch have switched to
MSDOS/Windows usage of carriage return character plus line feed,
so a file with 100 lines in it would be 100 bytes bigger.  That's
relatively unimportant *if* all the tools which use those files
can handle either format.


